Nina: opened a docs PR adding a small JS/TS streaming example for 429 fallback that resumes from KV prefix without double-emitting tokens. PR: https://git.redwood.ai/redwood/docs/pull/1367
Omar: nice idea. many examples show python; good to add a JS streaming playbook. can you show how to detect partial stream + do a short replay?
Nina: yep, sketch below. key idea: keep the last-sent token index in KV, request a short replay starting a few tokens before the gap to avoid duplication, and use a dedupe check on client side.
```ts
// pseudo-TS: stream with resume via KV prefix and conservative replay on 429
const prefix = await kv.get(sessionKey) // contains cached tokens + lastIndex
try {
  for await (const chunk of client.streamGenerate({ prompt, kvPrefix: prefix })) {
    ui.append(chunk.text)
    prefix.lastIndex = chunk.lastIndex // update local index mirror
  }
} catch (err) {
  if (err.code === 'TooManyRequests') {
    // short replay: request a small window starting a few tokens back
    const replayStart = Math.max(0, prefix.lastIndex - 6)
    const resp = await client.generate({ prompt, kvRange: [replayStart, prefix.lastIndex], maxTokens: 64 })
    // dedupe: drop the overlapping tokens that were already emitted
    const deduped = resp.text.slice(prefix.overlapCharCount)
    ui.append(deduped)
  } else {
    throw err
  }
}
```
